forbidden. Epic does not have any division and audio law that will be held in the world that fills all the courtyards such as planting fruit in the entire court and the High Court is forbidden and so forth from the PA:<br>
in order. He does not even have a partition sentence:<br>
And they compel. Shach wrote: even when there is no law of division, for although it is permitted for the mudar to enter, nevertheless we are concerned lest they use a forbidden use such as setting up an oven, etc.; but the view of Rashi and Tosafot is where it is permitted to enter the courtyard they do not compel; and it appears when they wish to divide or in a group, etc., they do as they wish. And the Ran wrote: specifically when one of them vowed they compel, but if both vowed they do not compel, for each one is reminded of his prohibition; but the Tur Choshen Mishpat siman 180 wrote that if they vowed benefit from each other and one of them is regular, they compel the regular one to sell; and the Yerushalmi explains that twice is called regular; also it implies there is no distinction between one who forbade himself from his fellow's share and one who forbade his fellow from his share — always they compel to sell; and the Bach wrote one must sell to another specifically, but to a partner it is forbidden to sell even though he said only "my courtyard," and his words are puzzling, etc. {Rama: but in Nekudot HaKesef he retracted and wrote his words are correct — see there.}
